{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FPyv9JYbeNMgawtdPPKnNa","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL Rolling Review review form 2025 overall assessment soundness excitement scale description\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \\\"Publish or Perish\\\" Circus | CSPaper Forum\",\"url\":\"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus\"},{\"title\":\"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/acguidelines.md at main · yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview/blob/main/acguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"ACL ARR 2025 May | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FMay\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ACL 2025 Statistics - Paper Copilot\",\"url\":\"https://papercopilot.com/statistics/acl-statistics/acl-2025-statistics/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) 2025 review form with descriptions of the overall assessment, soundness, and excitement scales:\n\n## Three Scoring Components\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Soundness Scale\n\nThe soundness question asks: \"Given that this is a short/long paper, is it sufficiently sound and thorough? Does it clearly state scientific claims and provide adequate support for them?\" The scale includes ratings such as:\n\n- 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n- 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n- 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n\n## Excitement Scale\n\nExcitement is subjective, and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field. Papers may be perceived as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions/methods/datasets/metrics, or reviewers may be excited about the possible impact of the paper on some community (not necessarily large or their own) by lowering barriers, reducing costs, enabling new applications. The scale includes:\n\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"4060a06d-b4f0-4898-b0ce-654ca7deba06","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:14:11.211Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL Rolling Review review form 2025 overall assessment soundness excitement scale description","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014Pn1VScAt2u6NS4JWhtiCh","content":[{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \"Publish or Perish\" Circus | CSPaper Forum","url":"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus"},{"title":"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023","url":"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/acguidelines.md at main · yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview/blob/main/acguidelines.md"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md"},{"title":"ACL ARR 2025 May | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FMay"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ACL 2025 Statistics - Paper Copilot","url":"https://papercopilot.com/statistics/acl-statistics/acl-2025-statistics/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) 2025 review form with descriptions of the overall assessment, soundness, and excitement scales:\n\n## Three Scoring Components\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Soundness Scale\n\nThe soundness question asks: \"Given that this is a short/long paper, is it sufficiently sound and thorough? Does it clearly state scientific claims and provide adequate support for them?\" The scale includes ratings such as:\n\n- 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n- 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n- 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n\n## Excitement Scale\n\nExcitement is subjective, and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field. Papers may be perceived as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions/methods/datasets/metrics, or reviewers may be excited about the possible impact of the paper on some community (not necessarily large or their own) by lowering barriers, reducing costs, enabling new applications. The scale includes:\n\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":8.051354369}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C7ZNweF3MJqBTNhyd6e1Kz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the materials. Let me fetch the ARR review form details more precisely, then proceed with the full review.\n\nLet me summarize what I know:\n\n**ARR/EMNLP Review Form (2025):**\n- Soundness (1-5): How thorough and well-supported are the claims\n- Excitement (1-5): How novel/impactful/surprising\n- Overall Assessment (1-5): Composite recommendation\n\nThe scales are:\n- 5 = Award-worthy / Top 2.5%\n- 4 = Strong / Would recommend\n\n\n- 3 = Acceptable / Borderline\n- 2 = Below threshold\n- 1 = Not suitable\n\n**Paper Summary:**\nThe paper investigates whether confidence signals from language models can effectively predict and select high-quality outputs in structured information extraction tasks like named entity recognition and relation extraction, using constrained decoding with multiple samples from Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B on benchmark datasets.\n\nThe core tension is that while structural consistency metrics like set Jaccard and Fleiss' kappa achieve strong quality estimation performance, they don't actually help select better samples than greedy decoding. Removing constrained decoding restores variance in log-probabilities and slightly improves selection for NER but not relation extraction. The findings also reveal that these structural signals degrade significantly on out-of-distribution data, while log-probability behaves inconsistently across domains. A supervised verifier can recover some of the gap between current selection and oracle performance, though it captures only a fraction of the potential improvement.\n\nNow I'm mapping out which experiments validate these findings against the paper's claims, and I see all four key ablations are already documented. I need to organize a formal review process with two independent reviewers examining different aspects—one focusing on methodology and experimental design, the other on novelty and positioning relative to prior work—then have them exchange perspectives and converge on a consolidated assessment.\n\nI'll set up both reviewers as parallel agents with detailed prompts covering everything I've analyzed from the paper, then facilitate the roundtable discussion myself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28532,"cache_read_input_tokens":27035,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":28532},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"a2058865-f4a3-4be4-b70c-83d8626c3ec5"}
