{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AV8C6ErHUydX2LGyfWm1JT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NH2gxqJvj9Pis35Mu3qGGz","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support, extras nice but not essential), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Below threshold, 1=Not suitable\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting, 3=Borderline, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact. 5=Award-worthy top 2.5%, 4=Strong accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Paper: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning, but effectiveness for structured IE under constrained decoding is unclear. Systematically evaluates 5 confidence signal families—log-probability, set Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' kappa (FK), exact match (EM), and voting confidence—for quality estimation (QE) and sample selection in NER and RE on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B. Finds a \"predict-but-cannot-select\" gap: structural consistency signals achieve strong QE (AUROC up to 0.75) yet fail to improve selection over greedy (best gain: +0.6pp, p=0.39). Provides evidence constrained decoding contributes to bottleneck: removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x, improves NER selection +2.0pp (p=0.078). RE shows minimal improvement. Supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom on NER.\n\n### Three Contributions\n1. **Predict-but-cannot-select gap**: Documents regime-specific selection bottleneck in constrained-decoding IE, with ablation evidence that constrained decoding contributes via logprob compression.\n2. **QE evaluation framework**: Systematic comparison of 5 signals across 2 tasks, 2 datasets, 2 models (AUROC up to 0.75).\n3. **Gap closability**: Supervised verifier captures 15.9% oracle headroom on NER, providing a baseline.\n\n### Method Details\n- **Task setup**: Fine-tuned LLM M under constrained (grammar-guided/Outlines) decoding generates N=8 candidates at T=1.0. Each y_i is JSON-formatted entity set or relation triples. Greedy decode y_0 at T=0.\n- **Two evaluation tasks**: (1) QE: predicting easy/hard instances; (2) Sample Selection: choosing best y_i from N candidates.\n- **5 signals**: Log-probability (per-sample, mean token-level logprob); SJ (instance-level, pairwise structural Jaccard with Hungarian matching); FK (instance-level, Fleiss' kappa treating entity mentions as categories); EM (instance-level, pairwise exact match fraction); Voting confidence (instance-level, entity-level voting fraction averaged).\n- **Key conceptual point**: 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates (cannot rank within instance). Only logprob is per-sample. This QE vs selection distinction is central.\n- **Free-form ablation** (§3.3): N=8 samples without Outlines constraints on SciERC. Parse rate: 83% NER, 78% RE. Paired comparison constrained vs free-form.\n- **Supervised verifier** (§3.4): DeBERTa-v3-large regression on (input, output) → sample F1. 5-fold CV. Features: token overlap, entity count difference, output length, JSON complexity.\n- **Metrics**: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC (binarized at median F1), ECE. Selection: F1 of signal-chosen candidate vs greedy/random/oracle. Bootstrap CIs (1000 iter) and paired Wilcoxon.\n\n### Experimental Setup\n- **Models**: Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, both fine-tuned with LoRA.\n- **Datasets**: SciERC (NER+RE), CoNLL-2003 (NER), WNUT-17 (OOD NER).\n- **Sampling**: N=8, T=1.0, Outlines constrained decoding.\n\n### Results (from paper)\n\n**§5.1 QE (Table 1, SciERC Qwen3-8B)**:\n- NER: voting_conf ρ=.379, SJ ρ=.360, EM ρ=.295, FK ρ=.267, logprob ρ=.205\n- AUROC: EM .749, SJ .692, voting .684, FK .629, logprob .578\n- ECE: EM .353, voting .381, FK .451, SJ .453, logprob .706\n- RE AUROC: EM .706, voting .694, SJ .670, FK .609, logprob .601\n\n**§5.2 Selection (Table 2, SciERC Qwen3-8B)**:\n- NER: oracle .776, logprob .650, SJ .645, greedy .644, voting .634, random .608\n- RE: oracle .585, greedy .404, logprob .395 (WORSE than greedy), SJ .386, voting .359, random .354\n- Logprob selection: +0.6pp NER (p=0.39, d=0.038), worse than greedy on RE\n- 60% NER instances have logprob range < 0.05 (effectively tied)\n- Non-tied 40%: +2.9pp (p=0.01)\n\n**§5.3 Free-form Ablation (Table 3)**:\n- Variance recovery: NER range 0.038→0.148 (3.9x), RE 0.032→0.118 (3.7x), Wilcoxon p<10^-48 / p<10^-31\n- Tied fraction: NER 60%→28%, RE 64%→33%\n- ρ improvement: NER logprob 0.205→0.298 (+.093), RE 0.266→0.292 (+.026)\n- AUROC: NER 0.578→0.623 (+4.5pp), RE 0.601→0.618 (+1.7pp)\n- Selection: NER free-form +2.0pp (p=0.078, d=0.125), constrained +0.6pp (p=0.39)\n- RE free-form +0.8pp (p=0.31) — minimal\n- Table 4: structural signals slightly degrade under free-form (SJ .360→.335, voting .379→.358)\n- Non-tied analysis: free-form 72% non-tied, +2.8pp (p=0.025); constrained 40% non-tied, +2.9pp (p=0.01) — comparable when logprob has variance\n\n**§5.4 Cross-model/dataset (Table 5)**:\n- 2×2 matrix: SJ ρ cross-model delta < 0.01 (Qwen SciERC .360, LLaMA SciERC .363; Qwen CoNLL .436, LLaMA CoNLL .431)\n- CoNLL ceiling: greedy F1=.926, oracle headroom 1.9pp, selection useless\n- Logprob AUROC reversal on CoNLL: logprob .761 > all structural signals (~.704) — explained as ceiling effect\n\n**§5.5 OOD (Table 7-8, WNUT-17)**:\n- Structural signals retain ~40% of in-domain ρ (voting .379→.165, SJ .360→.142)\n- Logprob inverts: .205→-.287\n- Selection nearly random (best voting .220 vs random .190)\n\n**§5.6 Verifier (Tables 9-10)**:\n- NER: AUROC .812±.031, selection F1 .665 (+2.1pp over greedy), 15.9% headroom capture\n- RE: AUROC .762±.038, selection F1 .421 (+1.8pp), 9.9% headroom capture\n- Feature importance: token overlap (0.31 NER, 0.28 RE) and entity count diff (0.22 NER) dominate\n- Headroom cascade: greedy 0% → best unsup 4.5% → verifier 15.9% → oracle 100% (NER)\n\n### Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment list includes 4 experiments: exp-011 (OOD NER), exp-017 (LLaMA CoNLL), exp-018 (free-form ablation), exp-019 (simple verifier). ALL appear covered in the paper.\n\n### Limitations Acknowledged\n- Only 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset\n- Only 8B models; larger models may have different logprob compression\n- Free-form parse rate confound (83% NER, 78% RE; parseable subset may not be representative)\n- Verifier trained on single LLM's samples, 5-fold CV variance large\n- Softened claims: \"evidence consistent with\" not \"experimentally verified\"\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences): What does the paper do and what does it find?\n\n2. **Soundness of methodology**: \n   - Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls appropriate?\n   - Are the 5 signals well-chosen and well-defined? Any missing important baselines?\n   - Is the free-form ablation well-controlled? How serious is the parse-rate confound?\n   - Is the verifier evaluation sound (5-fold CV, feature analysis)?\n   - Are statistical tests appropriate (bootstrap CI, Wilcoxon)?\n\n3. **Strength of empirical evidence**:\n   - Does the data support the three claims?\n   - How concerning is p=0.078 for the key NER selection result?\n   - How concerning is the RE non-result (p=0.31)?\n   - Is the cross-model validation (delta < 0.01) convincing?\n   - Is N=8, T=1.0 sufficiently explored? Should they vary N or T?\n\n4. **Weaknesses**:\n   - Missing experiments or analyses\n   - Threats to validity\n   - Over-claiming or under-claiming\n\n5. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions\n\n6. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5 with justification)\n   - Excitement (1-5 with justification)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5): How confident are you in your assessment?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is for EMNLP 2026 Findings (a venue that accepts solid empirical contributions that may not clear the main conference bar).\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":331,"cache_read_input_tokens":58895,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":331},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"0200e237-19c9-48f7-a7a2-45fb8ce370c4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a40626c9977ec7a5d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NH2gxqJvj9Pis35Mu3qGGz","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support, extras nice but not essential), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Below threshold, 1=Not suitable\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting, 3=Borderline, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact. 5=Award-worthy top 2.5%, 4=Strong accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Paper: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning, but effectiveness for structured IE under constrained decoding is unclear. Systematically evaluates 5 confidence signal families—log-probability, set Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' kappa (FK), exact match (EM), and voting confidence—for quality estimation (QE) and sample selection in NER and RE on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B. Finds a \"predict-but-cannot-select\" gap: structural consistency signals achieve strong QE (AUROC up to 0.75) yet fail to improve selection over greedy (best gain: +0.6pp, p=0.39). Provides evidence constrained decoding contributes to bottleneck: removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x, improves NER selection +2.0pp (p=0.078). RE shows minimal improvement. Supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom on NER.\n\n### Three Contributions\n1. **Predict-but-cannot-select gap**: Documents regime-specific selection bottleneck in constrained-decoding IE, with ablation evidence that constrained decoding contributes via logprob compression.\n2. **QE evaluation framework**: Systematic comparison of 5 signals across 2 tasks, 2 datasets, 2 models (AUROC up to 0.75).\n3. **Gap closability**: Supervised verifier captures 15.9% oracle headroom on NER, providing a baseline.\n\n### Method Details\n- **Task setup**: Fine-tuned LLM M under constrained (grammar-guided/Outlines) decoding generates N=8 candidates at T=1.0. Each y_i is JSON-formatted entity set or relation triples. Greedy decode y_0 at T=0.\n- **Two evaluation tasks**: (1) QE: predicting easy/hard instances; (2) Sample Selection: choosing best y_i from N candidates.\n- **5 signals**: Log-probability (per-sample, mean token-level logprob); SJ (instance-level, pairwise structural Jaccard with Hungarian matching); FK (instance-level, Fleiss' kappa treating entity mentions as categories); EM (instance-level, pairwise exact match fraction); Voting confidence (instance-level, entity-level voting fraction averaged).\n- **Key conceptual point**: 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates (cannot rank within instance). Only logprob is per-sample. This QE vs selection distinction is central.\n- **Free-form ablation** (§3.3): N=8 samples without Outlines constraints on SciERC. Parse rate: 83% NER, 78% RE. Paired comparison constrained vs free-form.\n- **Supervised verifier** (§3.4): DeBERTa-v3-large regression on (input, output) → sample F1. 5-fold CV. Features: token overlap, entity count difference, output length, JSON complexity.\n- **Metrics**: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC (binarized at median F1), ECE. Selection: F1 of signal-chosen candidate vs greedy/random/oracle. Bootstrap CIs (1000 iter) and paired Wilcoxon.\n\n### Experimental Setup\n- **Models**: Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, both fine-tuned with LoRA.\n- **Datasets**: SciERC (NER+RE), CoNLL-2003 (NER), WNUT-17 (OOD NER).\n- **Sampling**: N=8, T=1.0, Outlines constrained decoding.\n\n### Results (from paper)\n\n**§5.1 QE (Table 1, SciERC Qwen3-8B)**:\n- NER: voting_conf ρ=.379, SJ ρ=.360, EM ρ=.295, FK ρ=.267, logprob ρ=.205\n- AUROC: EM .749, SJ .692, voting .684, FK .629, logprob .578\n- ECE: EM .353, voting .381, FK .451, SJ .453, logprob .706\n- RE AUROC: EM .706, voting .694, SJ .670, FK .609, logprob .601\n\n**§5.2 Selection (Table 2, SciERC Qwen3-8B)**:\n- NER: oracle .776, logprob .650, SJ .645, greedy .644, voting .634, random .608\n- RE: oracle .585, greedy .404, logprob .395 (WORSE than greedy), SJ .386, voting .359, random .354\n- Logprob selection: +0.6pp NER (p=0.39, d=0.038), worse than greedy on RE\n- 60% NER instances have logprob range < 0.05 (effectively tied)\n- Non-tied 40%: +2.9pp (p=0.01)\n\n**§5.3 Free-form Ablation (Table 3)**:\n- Variance recovery: NER range 0.038→0.148 (3.9x), RE 0.032→0.118 (3.7x), Wilcoxon p<10^-48 / p<10^-31\n- Tied fraction: NER 60%→28%, RE 64%→33%\n- ρ improvement: NER logprob 0.205→0.298 (+.093), RE 0.266→0.292 (+.026)\n- AUROC: NER 0.578→0.623 (+4.5pp), RE 0.601→0.618 (+1.7pp)\n- Selection: NER free-form +2.0pp (p=0.078, d=0.125), constrained +0.6pp (p=0.39)\n- RE free-form +0.8pp (p=0.31) — minimal\n- Table 4: structural signals slightly degrade under free-form (SJ .360→.335, voting .379→.358)\n- Non-tied analysis: free-form 72% non-tied, +2.8pp (p=0.025); constrained 40% non-tied, +2.9pp (p=0.01) — comparable when logprob has variance\n\n**§5.4 Cross-model/dataset (Table 5)**:\n- 2×2 matrix: SJ ρ cross-model delta < 0.01 (Qwen SciERC .360, LLaMA SciERC .363; Qwen CoNLL .436, LLaMA CoNLL .431)\n- CoNLL ceiling: greedy F1=.926, oracle headroom 1.9pp, selection useless\n- Logprob AUROC reversal on CoNLL: logprob .761 > all structural signals (~.704) — explained as ceiling effect\n\n**§5.5 OOD (Table 7-8, WNUT-17)**:\n- Structural signals retain ~40% of in-domain ρ (voting .379→.165, SJ .360→.142)\n- Logprob inverts: .205→-.287\n- Selection nearly random (best voting .220 vs random .190)\n\n**§5.6 Verifier (Tables 9-10)**:\n- NER: AUROC .812±.031, selection F1 .665 (+2.1pp over greedy), 15.9% headroom capture\n- RE: AUROC .762±.038, selection F1 .421 (+1.8pp), 9.9% headroom capture\n- Feature importance: token overlap (0.31 NER, 0.28 RE) and entity count diff (0.22 NER) dominate\n- Headroom cascade: greedy 0% → best unsup 4.5% → verifier 15.9% → oracle 100% (NER)\n\n### Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment list includes 4 experiments: exp-011 (OOD NER), exp-017 (LLaMA CoNLL), exp-018 (free-form ablation), exp-019 (simple verifier). ALL appear covered in the paper.\n\n### Limitations Acknowledged\n- Only 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset\n- Only 8B models; larger models may have different logprob compression\n- Free-form parse rate confound (83% NER, 78% RE; parseable subset may not be representative)\n- Verifier trained on single LLM's samples, 5-fold CV variance large\n- Softened claims: \"evidence consistent with\" not \"experimentally verified\"\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences): What does the paper do and what does it find?\n\n2. **Soundness of methodology**: \n   - Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls appropriate?\n   - Are the 5 signals well-chosen and well-defined? Any missing important baselines?\n   - Is the free-form ablation well-controlled? How serious is the parse-rate confound?\n   - Is the verifier evaluation sound (5-fold CV, feature analysis)?\n   - Are statistical tests appropriate (bootstrap CI, Wilcoxon)?\n\n3. **Strength of empirical evidence**:\n   - Does the data support the three claims?\n   - How concerning is p=0.078 for the key NER selection result?\n   - How concerning is the RE non-result (p=0.31)?\n   - Is the cross-model validation (delta < 0.01) convincing?\n   - Is N=8, T=1.0 sufficiently explored? Should they vary N or T?\n\n4. **Weaknesses**:\n   - Missing experiments or analyses\n   - Threats to validity\n   - Over-claiming or under-claiming\n\n5. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions\n\n6. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5 with justification)\n   - Excitement (1-5 with justification)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5): How confident are you in your assessment?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is for EMNLP 2026 Findings (a venue that accepts solid empirical contributions that may not clear the main conference bar).\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers.","uuid":"307e9607-434f-4864-9a61-dc81b0907be6","session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91"}
