{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BoWD8nnkKFRYyTeUjLbx9y","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/main.bbl:29:  Plappert, Tworek, Hilton, Nakano, Hesse, and Schulman}]{cobbe2021verifiers}\ndocs/paper/main.bbl:33:\\newblock \\href {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.14168} {Training verifiers to solve\ndocs/paper/conclusion.tex:4:By construction, four of five signals assign identical scores to all candidates from the same input; log-probability, the only per-sample signal, also fails ($p{>}0.39$) despite 13--18~pp oracle headroom due to constrained-decoding-induced score compression.\ndocs/paper/conclusion.tex:9:Trained verifiers, relaxed decoding constraints, scaling to 70B+ models, and conformal prediction are promising directions for closing the gap.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:11:\\textit{Layer 2 (constrained-decoding compression):} Log-probability, the only per-sample signal, also fails to exploit the 13--18~pp oracle headroom.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:31:Effective selection likely requires either per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers, venktesh2025trust} or relaxation of the constrained-decoding regime that compresses LP resolution; $N$-scaling analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:additional}) shows $N{=}8$ captures most quality estimation benefit.\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:25:Conformal prediction \\cite{fisch2021conformal, angelopoulos2021conformal} provides coverage guarantees; trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify, venktesh2025trust} and reward models \\cite{wang2024mathshepherd} learn per-sample scoring.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:118:The non-trivial question is whether log-probability---the only per-sample signal---can exploit the oracle headroom.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:123:On NER, the oracle (0.776) reveals 13.2~pp of headroom above greedy (0.644).\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:125:On RE, the best signal (LP, 0.395) falls below greedy (0.404, $-0.9$\\,pp, $p{=}0.80$), while 18.1~pp of oracle headroom remains.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:133:This gap has direct consequences for test-time scaling in IE \\cite{brown2024monkeys, snell2024scaling}: closing it likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.\ndocs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex:4:By construction, four of five signals assign identical scores to all candidates from the same input; log-probability, the only per-sample signal, also fails ($p{>}0.39$) despite 13--18~pp oracle headroom due to score compression.\ndocs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex:9:Trained verifiers, scaling to 70B+ models, and conformal prediction are promising directions for closing the gap.\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:11:\\textit{Layer 2 (score compression):} Log-probability, the only per-sample signal, also fails to exploit the 13--18~pp oracle headroom.\ndocs/paper/sections/discussion.tex:36:Effective selection likely requires per-sample signals such as trained verifiers \\cite{cobbe2021verifiers, venktesh2025trust}; $N$-scaling analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:additional}) shows $N{=}8$ captures most quality estimation benefit.\ndocs/paper/sections/limitations.tex:10:We do not train a dedicated verifier or reward model, leaving unknown how much of the oracle headroom a supervised approach could recover.\ndocs/paper/sections/abstract.tex:4:These signals achieve strong quality estimation, yet none significantly improves sample selection over greedy decoding despite 13--18 percentage points of oracle headroom---a \\textit{correlation-selection gap}.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:264:Across SciERC configurations, 39--42\\% of instances contain at least one sample with F1 exceeding greedy: Qwen NER 42.0\\% (222/529, mean headroom $+$31.9~pp among improvable instances), Qwen RE 41.3\\% (164/397, $+$45.3~pp), LLaMA NER 39.3\\% (208/529, $+$33.7~pp).\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:268:On the 44\\% of Qwen SciERC instances with high diversity (${\\geq}4$ unique outputs among 8 samples, $n{=}232$), oracle headroom reaches $+$16.3~pp, yet all consistency-based signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) select at or below greedy, and LP---the only per-sample signal---achieves merely $+$0.8~pp.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:269:LLaMA replicates this pattern: on its high-diversity subset ($n{=}186$), oracle headroom reaches $+$20.1~pp while consistency signals remain at or below greedy and LP captures only $+$2.0~pp.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:275:The $\\delta$ values are anchored to oracle headroom (13--18~pp): $\\delta{=}5$~pp corresponds to forgoing 28--38\\% of the available headroom, $\\delta{=}3$~pp forgoing 17--23\\%, and $\\delta{=}2$~pp less than 15\\%.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:276:The breakpoint $\\delta^*$---the minimum equivalence margin at which each signal passes TOST---ranges from approximately 1.3~pp (SJ, ${\\sim}$10\\% of oracle headroom) to 3.4~pp (FK, ${\\sim}$26\\%) on NER, and from approximately 2.9~pp (LP, ${\\sim}$16\\%) to 6.5~pp (VC, ${\\sim}$36\\%) on RE.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:412:Oracle headroom exists (0.757 vs.\\ 0.674) but no signal exploits it, ruling out the 40\\% zero-F1 floor effect as the explanation for RE selection failure.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:22:Our central finding is the \\textbf{correlation-selection gap} (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}): consistency-based signals achieve strong quality estimation ($\\rho$ up to 0.38, AUROC up to 0.75) but fail at sample selection despite 13--18~pp of oracle headroom.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:24:The non-trivial questions are whether (a)~consensus-proximity selection---instantiating MBR decoding \\cite{eikema2022sampling, freitag2022high, muller2021understanding} with structural utility functions---can recover discrimination, and (b)~whether log-probability, the only per-sample signal, can exploit the headroom.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:39:    We demonstrate that strong quality estimation ($\\rho{=}0.38$, AUROC${=}0.75$) coexists with selection failure ($p{>}0.39$, 13--18~pp oracle headroom).\ndocs/paper/abstract.tex:4:These signals achieve strong quality estimation, yet none significantly improves sample selection over greedy decoding despite 13--18 percentage points of oracle headroom.\ndocs/paper/references.bib:27:@article{cobbe2021verifiers,\ndocs/paper/references.bib:28:  title = {Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems},\ndocs/paper/main.aux:245:\\citation{cobbe2021verifiers,lightman2024verify,venktesh2025trust}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:583:\\citation{cobbe2021verifiers}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:757:\\citation{cobbe2021verifiers,venktesh2025trust}\ndocs/paper/main.aux:867:\\bibcite{cobbe2021verifiers}{{4}{2021}{{Cobbe et~al.}}{{Cobbe, Kosaraju, Bavarian, Chen, Jun, Kaiser, Plappert, Tworek, Hilton, Nakano, Hesse, and Schulman}}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NH2gxqJvj9Pis35Mu3qGGz","session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"2896977d-258b-40ca-8340-94ab9fd1bc28","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:16:29.489Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AV8C6ErHUydX2LGyfWm1JT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G7tZFLmL7vDnamgjTBwoNu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Below threshold, 1=Not suitable\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Borderline, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite. 5=Award-worthy, 4=Strong accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Paper: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning, but effectiveness for structured IE under constrained decoding is unclear. Evaluates 5 confidence signals—log-probability, set Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' kappa (FK), exact match (EM), voting confidence—for QE and sample selection in NER and RE on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B. Finds \"predict-but-cannot-select\" gap: structural consistency signals achieve strong QE (AUROC up to 0.75) yet fail to improve selection (best: +0.6pp, p=0.39). Constrained decoding contributes: removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x, improves NER selection +2.0pp (p=0.078). RE shows minimal improvement. Supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom.\n\n### Three Contributions\n1. **Predict-but-cannot-select gap**: Regime-specific selection bottleneck in constrained-decoding IE.\n2. **QE evaluation framework**: Systematic 5-signal comparison across 2 tasks, 2 datasets, 2 models.\n3. **Gap closability**: Supervised verifier baseline capturing 15.9% headroom.\n\n### Related Work (from paper §2)\nThe paper positions itself across several areas:\n\n**Test-time scaling and sample selection**: Cites self-consistency [Wang et al. 2023], CISC [2025], Self-Certainty [2026], ReASC [2026], Ranked Voting SC [2025], Snell et al. [2024], Brown et al. [2024]. Claims: \"All of these operate on discrete answers in reasoning/QA settings; none address structured IE outputs.\"\n\n**Uncertainty quantification for LLMs**: Cites semantic entropy [Kuhn et al. 2023], Kadavath et al. [2022], Guo et al. [2017], SelfCheckGPT [Manakul et al. 2023], Semantic Probes [2024], multiple surveys [UQ Survey 2025, UQ+Calib Survey 2025, Confidence Survey 2024], selective prediction [Geifman and El-Yaniv 2017, Know Your Limits 2025], CoCoA [Vashurin et al. 2025a].\n\n**Consistency for structured IE**: Cites Nédellec and Xia [2026] (consistency-accuracy correlation for plant health NER/RE with surface-level matching—\"overlooks semantically consistent outputs\"), Atomic SC [2024] (long-form text decomposition), Latent SC [2025] (embedding-space). Claims neither handles set-structured IE outputs.\n\n**Structured prediction confidence**: Conformal prediction [Fisch 2021, Angelopoulos 2021, Bates 2021], token-level NER uncertainty [Culotta and McCallum 2004, Xiao and Wang 2019, Gal and Ghahramani 2016, Shelmanov et al. 2021, Lakshminarayanan et al. 2017], pre-trained model calibration [Desai and Durrett 2020], structured output calibration [Jagannatha and Yu 2022], verifiers [Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2023].\n\n**MBR decoding**: [Eikema and Aziz 2022]. Claims set-based IE metrics create different optimization landscape where consensus-proximity fails.\n\n### Method Details\n- 5 signals: logprob (per-sample), SJ/FK/EM/voting (all instance-level aggregates).\n- Key conceptual point: 4/5 signals are instance-level (cannot rank candidates within an instance). Only logprob can select. The paper argues this QE-vs-selection distinction has been \"conflated in prior work on self-consistency for IE.\"\n- Free-form decoding ablation removes Outlines constraints.\n- DeBERTa verifier as supervised upper bound.\n\n### Key Results Summary\n- QE: Structural signals outperform logprob on ρ (voting .379 vs logprob .205 on SciERC NER), but AUROC shows EM at .749 (NER) / .706 (RE).\n- Selection: Nothing beats greedy significantly (best: logprob +0.6pp, p=0.39). Oracle headroom exists (NER .776, RE .585).\n- Free-form ablation: Logprob variance recovers 3.5-4x, NER selection improves +2.0pp (p=0.078), RE unchanged.\n- Cross-model: SJ ρ delta < 0.01 between Qwen and LLaMA.\n- OOD: Structural signals retain ~40%, logprob inverts to negative.\n- Verifier: 15.9% NER headroom capture, 9.9% RE.\n\n### References (30+ cited)\nWang et al. 2023 (self-consistency), Eikema and Aziz 2022 (MBR), Willard and Louf 2023 (Outlines), Kuhn et al. 2023 (semantic entropy), Kadavath et al. 2022, Guo et al. 2017, Manakul et al. 2023 (SelfCheckGPT), Luan et al. 2018 (SciERC), Tjong Kim Sang 2003 (CoNLL), Derczynski et al. 2017 (WNUT), Snell et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2024, CISC 2025, Self-Certainty 2026, ReASC 2026, Atomic SC 2024, Nédellec and Xia 2026, Fisch et al. 2021, Angelopoulos et al. 2021, Bates et al. 2021, Xiao and Wang 2019, Gal and Ghahramani 2016, Shelmanov et al. 2021, Lakshminarayanan et al. 2017, Desai and Durrett 2020, Jagannatha and Yu 2022, Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2023, Culotta and McCallum 2004, Semantic Probes 2024, various surveys, CoCoA [Vashurin 2025a], Latent SC 2025, Ranked Voting 2025, Kuleshov and Liang 2015, He et al. 2020.\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n2. **Novelty assessment**:\n   - How novel is the \"predict-but-cannot-select\" finding? Is it surprising or expected?\n   - How novel is the QE-vs-selection conceptual distinction for structured IE?\n   - How novel is the free-form ablation as a diagnostic tool?\n   - How novel is the verifier baseline approach?\n   - Is this an incremental extension of self-consistency to a new domain, or does it offer genuine new insight?\n\n3. **Related work coverage**:\n   - Are the key related works cited and properly positioned?\n   - Any critical missing references? (Think: structured output self-consistency, MBR for structured prediction, constrained decoding analysis, calibration under grammar constraints)\n   - Is the claim \"none address structured IE\" accurate?\n   - How does this compare to Nédellec and Xia [2026]? Is the differentiation convincing?\n   - Is CoCoA [Vashurin et al. 2025a] sufficiently discussed?\n\n4. **Framing and positioning**:\n   - Is the paper well-framed for EMNLP Findings?\n   - Is the \"predict-but-cannot-select\" terminology clear and useful?\n   - Are the three contributions well-scoped for the evidence?\n   - Is the title accurate and informative?\n\n5. **Potential impact**:\n   - Who would benefit from this work?\n   - Does it open productive research directions?\n   - Is it likely to be cited?\n\n6. **Weaknesses**:\n   - Novelty limitations\n   - Missing context or positioning issues\n   - Over-claiming or framing concerns\n\n7. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions\n\n8. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5 with justification)\n   - Excitement (1-5 with justification)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. EMNLP Findings accepts solid empirical work that may not be main-conference level but provides useful contributions to the community.\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":331,"cache_read_input_tokens":58895,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":331},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"9e8be594-7b30-4a46-8fac-9a11ee7e9dd3"}
