{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NH2gxqJvj9Pis35Mu3qGGz","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review based on thorough reading of the complete paper (all sections including appendix).\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n**Note on discrepancy**: The abstract provided in the review request describes results (DeBERTa verifier capturing 15.9% headroom, free-form ablation recovering logprob variance ~3.5x) that do NOT appear in the submitted paper. The actual paper's free-form ablation shows the opposite finding (variance ratio 0.97, virtually unchanged), and the paper explicitly states in Limitations: \"We do not train a dedicated verifier or reward model.\" The paper's actual title is \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\" I review the paper as actually written.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper systematically evaluates five confidence signals from three families (structural, surface, model-internal) for quality estimation and sample selection in LLM-based NER and RE, using two 8B models across three benchmarks. The central finding is a \"correlation-selection gap\": signals that predict instance-level quality well (AUROC up to 0.75) fail to select better candidates from N=8 samples, with log-probability--the only per-sample signal--compressed into effective ties for 60% of instances. A controlled free-form decoding ablation confirms this compression is intrinsic to the model/task rather than an artifact of constrained decoding.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Soundness of Methodology\n\n**Experimental design**: Generally rigorous. The 2-task x 3-dataset x 2-model design provides reasonable coverage. The separation of quality estimation (instance-level) from sample selection (per-sample) is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution and is well-motivated. Controls are appropriate: greedy baseline, random baseline, oracle upper bound, bootstrap CIs, TOST equivalence testing, multi-seed stability analysis.\n\n**Signal selection**: The five signals are well-chosen and span a reasonable space. The structural/surface/model-internal taxonomy is clean. EM, SJ, FK, VC, and LP cover the natural design space for unsupervised confidence signals on set-valued outputs. One notable omission: **no sequence-level or generation-level entropy signal** is tested (e.g., predictive entropy, semantic entropy adapted to structured outputs). Given that log-probability is the sole per-sample signal and is the key bottleneck, testing entropy-based variants would strengthen the claim that model-internal signals generically fail.\n\n**Free-form ablation**: Well-controlled in principle (same model, prompts, temperature, differing only in decoding regime). The 99.95% parse rate is reassuring and removes the parse-rate confound that would have plagued this comparison. The finding that LP variance is virtually unchanged (std ratio 0.97) is clean and convincing. However, this ablation is conducted on only one dataset (SciERC) with one model (Qwen3-8B). The authors acknowledge this limitation. The ablation is the paper's most important mechanistic result and would benefit from replication on at least one additional configuration.\n\n**Statistical methodology**: Appropriate and thorough. Bootstrap CIs (B=10,000), Wilcoxon tests, TOST equivalence testing, Cohen's d, multi-seed stability--this is above-average statistical rigor for an NLP paper. The TOST analysis anchored to oracle headroom fractions is a nice touch.\n\n**Concerns**:\n- The FK signal assumes independent raters, which temperature-sampled outputs from the same model are not. The authors address this with an excess agreement analysis showing mild negative dependence, which is adequate but not fully rigorous--the dependence structure is more complex than what a mean excess agreement statistic captures.\n- The SJ metric uses Hungarian matching with token-level IoU, which is well-defined, but the choice of max(|g_i|, |g_j|) vs. |g_i union g_j| as normalizer is not justified beyond \"penalizes length asymmetry.\" An ablation on normalizer choice would strengthen the claim that SJ is the right structural metric.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Strength of Empirical Evidence\n\n**Claim 1 (Correlation-selection gap)**: Strongly supported for NER. The combination of p=0.39 for the best signal, Cohen's d=0.038, bootstrap CI spanning zero, and TOST equivalence at +/-2pp for the best signals is convincing evidence of a null result. The diagnostic decomposition into \"definitional\" (4/5 signals are instance-level) and \"score compression\" (LP tied for 60% of instances) is insightful and well-evidenced. The non-tied subset analysis (+2.9pp, p=0.01) provides a clean mechanistic confirmation.\n\nFor RE, the evidence is weaker. The paper relies on a single dataset (SciERC) and the 40% zero-F1 rate creates a severe floor effect. The authors partially address this with conditional selection analysis (Appendix Table 8), showing failure persists even on non-zero instances. This is adequate but not fully convincing given n=1 benchmark.\n\n**Claim 2 (QE evaluation framework)**: Well-supported. The rho-AUROC dissociation, difficulty-dependent signal rankings, conditional filtering analysis, and cross-model stability (delta < 0.01 on CoNLL) are all cleanly demonstrated. The WNUT-17 analysis revealing LP negative correlation and conditional collapse is a genuinely interesting finding.\n\n**Claim 3 (Task-complexity boundary)**: Reasonably supported but rests heavily on interpretation. The three-dataset difficulty gradient (CoNLL easy -> SciERC hard -> WNUT-17 boundary) provides suggestive evidence, but these datasets differ in many dimensions beyond \"difficulty\" (domain, schema complexity, annotation style, dataset size). The EM-leads-easy / SJ-leads-hard crossover is observed but not tested for significance.\n\n**Specific concerns on evidence strength**:\n\n- **p=0.078 is not in the paper**: The task description mentions p=0.078 for free-form NER selection, but the paper reports +0.18pp for free-form NER selection, and the ablation section does not report a p-value for this comparison. The paper's framing is correct in not over-interpreting this.\n\n- **Cross-model validation**: The delta < 0.01 result (SJ rho 0.436 vs 0.431 on CoNLL) is impressive but occurs on the easiest dataset where all signals converge. On SciERC, the multi-seed analysis shows LLaMA absolute values are \"systematically lower\" with larger sigma (0.021 vs 0.018 for SJ). The paper could be more explicit about the model-dependent magnitude caveat.\n\n- **N=8, T=1.0**: Only these primary settings are tested for selection. T=0.7 is tested for QE but not selection. N=16 is tested for QE but selection results are limited to cross-configuration tables showing the same null. The paper should discuss whether higher T or much larger N might unlock selection--the theoretical argument that more diversity could help is not addressed.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Missing supervised baseline in the main paper.** The paper identifies a 13-18pp oracle headroom gap but does not test whether ANY approach can close it. The Limitations section acknowledges \"We do not train a dedicated verifier or reward model.\" For a paper whose central narrative is \"these signals can't select,\" demonstrating what CAN select--even a simple supervised approach--would substantially strengthen the contribution. Without this, the paper documents a problem but offers no evidence about whether it is solvable.\n\n**W2. Single RE benchmark.** The paper repeatedly flags RE results as \"preliminary, single benchmark\" (appearing 5+ times). This is honest but limits the generalizability of approximately one-third of the claimed contributions. The RE conditional collapse, the LP-RE interaction in free-form decoding, and the RE miscalibration findings all rest on SciERC RE alone.\n\n**W3. Free-form ablation on single configuration.** The ablation is the paper's most important mechanistic finding, yet it covers only Qwen3-8B on SciERC. The RE interaction effect (LP selection flipping from -0.68pp to +1.69pp) is reported but unexplained, and without a second configuration, it is unclear whether this is a robust task-specific effect or noise.\n\n**W4. No discussion of when or why consistency signals succeed in MT/reasoning but fail here.** The MBR decoding connection is mentioned but not analyzed deeply. What structural property of IE outputs (as opposed to MT outputs) causes utility-quality misalignment within instances? Is it the discrete set structure? The combinatorial output space? The error correlation structure? The paper gestures at this (\"utility correlates with F1 across instances but not within them\") but does not provide a formal or empirical analysis of WHY.\n\n**W5. Somewhat predictable negative result.** For fine-tuned models generating near-identical structured outputs under constrained decoding, the finding that signals can't distinguish among very similar candidates is not highly surprising. The authors argue non-obviousness by analogy to MBR success in MT, but the analogy is imperfect (MT outputs are sequences with continuous similarity, not discrete sets). The paper would benefit from a stronger articulation of what specific prior work would have predicted success here.\n\n**W6. Title overclaims relative to evidence.** \"Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\" is a strong universal claim. The paper tests 2 models at 8B scale, 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset, and 1 decoding temperature. \"Cannot\" is too strong given this scope. \"Struggle to\" or \"fail to\" would be more appropriate.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Entropy-based signals**: Have you considered testing predictive entropy (sum of token-level entropies) or normalized entropy as an alternative per-sample signal? LP uses mean log-probability, but entropy captures uncertainty more directly and may be less compressed. If you have this data (since you collect token-level log-probs), this would be a low-cost but high-value addition.\n\n2. **Within-instance utility-quality correlation**: You state that MBR utility correlates with F1 across instances but not within them. Can you quantify the within-instance rank correlation between SJ-based consensus proximity score and per-sample F1? A distribution of within-instance Kendall tau values would make this claim precise.\n\n3. **Why does RE LP selection flip in free-form?** The constrained-to-free-form flip from -0.68pp to +1.69pp on RE is interesting but unexplained. Do you have any analysis of what changes structurally in RE outputs under free-form decoding? Is it output diversity, error type distribution, or something else?\n\n4. **Larger N for selection**: Your N-scaling analysis focuses on QE. At N=64 or N=128, the oracle headroom would be substantially larger. Would LP selection improve with more candidates, or does the compression bottleneck remain? Even a simulation (subsampling from N=16 to estimate N=64 behavior) would be informative.\n\n5. **The enable_thinking confound**: You acknowledge this for SciERC Qwen training. Is the greedy baseline (T=0) also affected? If the model was trained with empty think blocks but inference suppresses them, could this systematic train-test mismatch contribute to the compression effect? A simple diagnostic: does removing the SciERC Qwen configuration change any qualitative conclusions?\n\n---\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n**Soundness: 4 (Strong)**\nThe experimental design is rigorous, controls are appropriate, and statistical methodology is above average. The five signals are well-defined and the QE/selection separation is well-motivated. The free-form ablation is well-controlled. Weaknesses (single RE benchmark, single ablation configuration, missing supervised baseline) prevent a 5, but the core methodology is sound and the claims are appropriately hedged.\n\n**Excitement: 3 (Borderline)**\nThe correlation-selection gap is a useful conceptual contribution, and the systematic evaluation is thorough. However, the core finding--that unsupervised signals can't select among similar candidates from a fine-tuned model under constrained decoding--is moderately predictable. The paper documents a problem well but does not advance toward a solution. The difficulty-dependent signal ranking and WNUT-17 findings add interest but are secondary. For EMNLP Findings, this is adequate; for the main conference, the novelty bar would be tight.\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.5 (Leaning Accept)**\nA solid empirical contribution with thorough methodology, appropriate statistical rigor, and honest hedging of claims. The correlation-selection gap is a useful finding for the IE community, even if not highly surprising. The systematic signal comparison and difficulty-dependent analysis provide practical value. The paper is well-written (clear, well-organized, good use of appendix for detail). Weaknesses are primarily in scope (1 RE dataset, 1 ablation config, no supervised baseline) rather than methodology. Appropriate for EMNLP 2026 Findings as a careful empirical study that establishes an important negative result.\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\nI have read the full paper including all appendix sections, verified claims against reported numbers, and have relevant expertise in LLM-based IE and confidence estimation. I am less certain about the RE findings given the single-benchmark limitation, and I acknowledge uncertainty about whether larger models or different decoding strategies would alter the conclusions.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n- The conclusion file (`conclusion.tex`) and the `sections/conclusion.tex` differ: one mentions \"constrained-decoding-induced score compression\" and \"relaxed decoding constraints,\" while the other (correctly, per the ablation) says \"score compression\" without attributing it to constrained decoding. Ensure consistency.\n- Table 2 caption says \"RE results are from a single benchmark (preliminary)\" -- this caveat appears so frequently it becomes repetitive. State it once prominently and refer back.\n- The paper would benefit from a figure showing the LP score distribution (histogram of within-instance LP ranges) to make the compression claim visually immediate."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a40626c9977ec7a5d (use SendMessage with to: 'a40626c9977ec7a5d' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 64110\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 142592</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"01e14844-7cea-4363-aa63-61a4ed0b260e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:18:12.619Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient support, extras nice but not essential), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Below threshold, 1=Not suitable\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting (recommend to others), 4=Exciting, 3=Borderline, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact. 5=Award-worthy top 2.5%, 4=Strong accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Paper: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning, but effectiveness for structured IE under constrained decoding is unclear. Systematically evaluates 5 confidence signal families—log-probability, set Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' kappa (FK), exact match (EM), and voting confidence—for quality estimation (QE) and sample selection in NER and RE on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B. Finds a \"predict-but-cannot-select\" gap: structural consistency signals achieve strong QE (AUROC up to 0.75) yet fail to improve selection over greedy (best gain: +0.6pp, p=0.39). Provides evidence constrained decoding contributes to bottleneck: removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x, improves NER selection +2.0pp (p=0.078). RE shows minimal improvement. Supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom on NER.\n\n### Three Contributions\n1. **Predict-but-cannot-select gap**: Documents regime-specific selection bottleneck in constrained-decoding IE, with ablation evidence that constrained decoding contributes via logprob compression.\n2. **QE evaluation framework**: Systematic comparison of 5 signals across 2 tasks, 2 datasets, 2 models (AUROC up to 0.75).\n3. **Gap closability**: Supervised verifier captures 15.9% oracle headroom on NER, providing a baseline.\n\n### Method Details\n- **Task setup**: Fine-tuned LLM M under constrained (grammar-guided/Outlines) decoding generates N=8 candidates at T=1.0. Each y_i is JSON-formatted entity set or relation triples. Greedy decode y_0 at T=0.\n- **Two evaluation tasks**: (1) QE: predicting easy/hard instances; (2) Sample Selection: choosing best y_i from N candidates.\n- **5 signals**: Log-probability (per-sample, mean token-level logprob); SJ (instance-level, pairwise structural Jaccard with Hungarian matching); FK (instance-level, Fleiss' kappa treating entity mentions as categories); EM (instance-level, pairwise exact match fraction); Voting confidence (instance-level, entity-level voting fraction averaged).\n- **Key conceptual point**: 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates (cannot rank within instance). Only logprob is per-sample. This QE vs selection distinction is central.\n- **Free-form ablation** (§3.3): N=8 samples without Outlines constraints on SciERC. Parse rate: 83% NER, 78% RE. Paired comparison constrained vs free-form.\n- **Supervised verifier** (§3.4): DeBERTa-v3-large regression on (input, output) → sample F1. 5-fold CV. Features: token overlap, entity count difference, output length, JSON complexity.\n- **Metrics**: Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, AUROC (binarized at median F1), ECE. Selection: F1 of signal-chosen candidate vs greedy/random/oracle. Bootstrap CIs (1000 iter) and paired Wilcoxon.\n\n### Experimental Setup\n- **Models**: Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, both fine-tuned with LoRA.\n- **Datasets**: SciERC (NER+RE), CoNLL-2003 (NER), WNUT-17 (OOD NER).\n- **Sampling**: N=8, T=1.0, Outlines constrained decoding.\n\n### Results (from paper)\n\n**§5.1 QE (Table 1, SciERC Qwen3-8B)**:\n- NER: voting_conf ρ=.379, SJ ρ=.360, EM ρ=.295, FK ρ=.267, logprob ρ=.205\n- AUROC: EM .749, SJ .692, voting .684, FK .629, logprob .578\n- ECE: EM .353, voting .381, FK .451, SJ .453, logprob .706\n- RE AUROC: EM .706, voting .694, SJ .670, FK .609, logprob .601\n\n**§5.2 Selection (Table 2, SciERC Qwen3-8B)**:\n- NER: oracle .776, logprob .650, SJ .645, greedy .644, voting .634, random .608\n- RE: oracle .585, greedy .404, logprob .395 (WORSE than greedy), SJ .386, voting .359, random .354\n- Logprob selection: +0.6pp NER (p=0.39, d=0.038), worse than greedy on RE\n- 60% NER instances have logprob range < 0.05 (effectively tied)\n- Non-tied 40%: +2.9pp (p=0.01)\n\n**§5.3 Free-form Ablation (Table 3)**:\n- Variance recovery: NER range 0.038→0.148 (3.9x), RE 0.032→0.118 (3.7x), Wilcoxon p<10^-48 / p<10^-31\n- Tied fraction: NER 60%→28%, RE 64%→33%\n- ρ improvement: NER logprob 0.205→0.298 (+.093), RE 0.266→0.292 (+.026)\n- AUROC: NER 0.578→0.623 (+4.5pp), RE 0.601→0.618 (+1.7pp)\n- Selection: NER free-form +2.0pp (p=0.078, d=0.125), constrained +0.6pp (p=0.39)\n- RE free-form +0.8pp (p=0.31) — minimal\n- Table 4: structural signals slightly degrade under free-form (SJ .360→.335, voting .379→.358)\n- Non-tied analysis: free-form 72% non-tied, +2.8pp (p=0.025); constrained 40% non-tied, +2.9pp (p=0.01) — comparable when logprob has variance\n\n**§5.4 Cross-model/dataset (Table 5)**:\n- 2×2 matrix: SJ ρ cross-model delta < 0.01 (Qwen SciERC .360, LLaMA SciERC .363; Qwen CoNLL .436, LLaMA CoNLL .431)\n- CoNLL ceiling: greedy F1=.926, oracle headroom 1.9pp, selection useless\n- Logprob AUROC reversal on CoNLL: logprob .761 > all structural signals (~.704) — explained as ceiling effect\n\n**§5.5 OOD (Table 7-8, WNUT-17)**:\n- Structural signals retain ~40% of in-domain ρ (voting .379→.165, SJ .360→.142)\n- Logprob inverts: .205→-.287\n- Selection nearly random (best voting .220 vs random .190)\n\n**§5.6 Verifier (Tables 9-10)**:\n- NER: AUROC .812±.031, selection F1 .665 (+2.1pp over greedy), 15.9% headroom capture\n- RE: AUROC .762±.038, selection F1 .421 (+1.8pp), 9.9% headroom capture\n- Feature importance: token overlap (0.31 NER, 0.28 RE) and entity count diff (0.22 NER) dominate\n- Headroom cascade: greedy 0% → best unsup 4.5% → verifier 15.9% → oracle 100% (NER)\n\n### Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment list includes 4 experiments: exp-011 (OOD NER), exp-017 (LLaMA CoNLL), exp-018 (free-form ablation), exp-019 (simple verifier). ALL appear covered in the paper.\n\n### Limitations Acknowledged\n- Only 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset\n- Only 8B models; larger models may have different logprob compression\n- Free-form parse rate confound (83% NER, 78% RE; parseable subset may not be representative)\n- Verifier trained on single LLM's samples, 5-fold CV variance large\n- Softened claims: \"evidence consistent with\" not \"experimentally verified\"\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences): What does the paper do and what does it find?\n\n2. **Soundness of methodology**: \n   - Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls appropriate?\n   - Are the 5 signals well-chosen and well-defined? Any missing important baselines?\n   - Is the free-form ablation well-controlled? How serious is the parse-rate confound?\n   - Is the verifier evaluation sound (5-fold CV, feature analysis)?\n   - Are statistical tests appropriate (bootstrap CI, Wilcoxon)?\n\n3. **Strength of empirical evidence**:\n   - Does the data support the three claims?\n   - How concerning is p=0.078 for the key NER selection result?\n   - How concerning is the RE non-result (p=0.31)?\n   - Is the cross-model validation (delta < 0.01) convincing?\n   - Is N=8, T=1.0 sufficiently explored? Should they vary N or T?\n\n4. **Weaknesses**:\n   - Missing experiments or analyses\n   - Threats to validity\n   - Over-claiming or under-claiming\n\n5. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions\n\n6. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5 with justification)\n   - Excitement (1-5 with justification)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5): How confident are you in your assessment?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is for EMNLP 2026 Findings (a venue that accepts solid empirical contributions that may not clear the main conference bar).\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers.","agentId":"a40626c9977ec7a5d","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review based on thorough reading of the complete paper (all sections including appendix).\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n**Note on discrepancy**: The abstract provided in the review request describes results (DeBERTa verifier capturing 15.9% headroom, free-form ablation recovering logprob variance ~3.5x) that do NOT appear in the submitted paper. The actual paper's free-form ablation shows the opposite finding (variance ratio 0.97, virtually unchanged), and the paper explicitly states in Limitations: \"We do not train a dedicated verifier or reward model.\" The paper's actual title is \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\" I review the paper as actually written.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper systematically evaluates five confidence signals from three families (structural, surface, model-internal) for quality estimation and sample selection in LLM-based NER and RE, using two 8B models across three benchmarks. The central finding is a \"correlation-selection gap\": signals that predict instance-level quality well (AUROC up to 0.75) fail to select better candidates from N=8 samples, with log-probability--the only per-sample signal--compressed into effective ties for 60% of instances. A controlled free-form decoding ablation confirms this compression is intrinsic to the model/task rather than an artifact of constrained decoding.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Soundness of Methodology\n\n**Experimental design**: Generally rigorous. The 2-task x 3-dataset x 2-model design provides reasonable coverage. The separation of quality estimation (instance-level) from sample selection (per-sample) is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution and is well-motivated. Controls are appropriate: greedy baseline, random baseline, oracle upper bound, bootstrap CIs, TOST equivalence testing, multi-seed stability analysis.\n\n**Signal selection**: The five signals are well-chosen and span a reasonable space. The structural/surface/model-internal taxonomy is clean. EM, SJ, FK, VC, and LP cover the natural design space for unsupervised confidence signals on set-valued outputs. One notable omission: **no sequence-level or generation-level entropy signal** is tested (e.g., predictive entropy, semantic entropy adapted to structured outputs). Given that log-probability is the sole per-sample signal and is the key bottleneck, testing entropy-based variants would strengthen the claim that model-internal signals generically fail.\n\n**Free-form ablation**: Well-controlled in principle (same model, prompts, temperature, differing only in decoding regime). The 99.95% parse rate is reassuring and removes the parse-rate confound that would have plagued this comparison. The finding that LP variance is virtually unchanged (std ratio 0.97) is clean and convincing. However, this ablation is conducted on only one dataset (SciERC) with one model (Qwen3-8B). The authors acknowledge this limitation. The ablation is the paper's most important mechanistic result and would benefit from replication on at least one additional configuration.\n\n**Statistical methodology**: Appropriate and thorough. Bootstrap CIs (B=10,000), Wilcoxon tests, TOST equivalence testing, Cohen's d, multi-seed stability--this is above-average statistical rigor for an NLP paper. The TOST analysis anchored to oracle headroom fractions is a nice touch.\n\n**Concerns**:\n- The FK signal assumes independent raters, which temperature-sampled outputs from the same model are not. The authors address this with an excess agreement analysis showing mild negative dependence, which is adequate but not fully rigorous--the dependence structure is more complex than what a mean excess agreement statistic captures.\n- The SJ metric uses Hungarian matching with token-level IoU, which is well-defined, but the choice of max(|g_i|, |g_j|) vs. |g_i union g_j| as normalizer is not justified beyond \"penalizes length asymmetry.\" An ablation on normalizer choice would strengthen the claim that SJ is the right structural metric.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Strength of Empirical Evidence\n\n**Claim 1 (Correlation-selection gap)**: Strongly supported for NER. The combination of p=0.39 for the best signal, Cohen's d=0.038, bootstrap CI spanning zero, and TOST equivalence at +/-2pp for the best signals is convincing evidence of a null result. The diagnostic decomposition into \"definitional\" (4/5 signals are instance-level) and \"score compression\" (LP tied for 60% of instances) is insightful and well-evidenced. The non-tied subset analysis (+2.9pp, p=0.01) provides a clean mechanistic confirmation.\n\nFor RE, the evidence is weaker. The paper relies on a single dataset (SciERC) and the 40% zero-F1 rate creates a severe floor effect. The authors partially address this with conditional selection analysis (Appendix Table 8), showing failure persists even on non-zero instances. This is adequate but not fully convincing given n=1 benchmark.\n\n**Claim 2 (QE evaluation framework)**: Well-supported. The rho-AUROC dissociation, difficulty-dependent signal rankings, conditional filtering analysis, and cross-model stability (delta < 0.01 on CoNLL) are all cleanly demonstrated. The WNUT-17 analysis revealing LP negative correlation and conditional collapse is a genuinely interesting finding.\n\n**Claim 3 (Task-complexity boundary)**: Reasonably supported but rests heavily on interpretation. The three-dataset difficulty gradient (CoNLL easy -> SciERC hard -> WNUT-17 boundary) provides suggestive evidence, but these datasets differ in many dimensions beyond \"difficulty\" (domain, schema complexity, annotation style, dataset size). The EM-leads-easy / SJ-leads-hard crossover is observed but not tested for significance.\n\n**Specific concerns on evidence strength**:\n\n- **p=0.078 is not in the paper**: The task description mentions p=0.078 for free-form NER selection, but the paper reports +0.18pp for free-form NER selection, and the ablation section does not report a p-value for this comparison. The paper's framing is correct in not over-interpreting this.\n\n- **Cross-model validation**: The delta < 0.01 result (SJ rho 0.436 vs 0.431 on CoNLL) is impressive but occurs on the easiest dataset where all signals converge. On SciERC, the multi-seed analysis shows LLaMA absolute values are \"systematically lower\" with larger sigma (0.021 vs 0.018 for SJ). The paper could be more explicit about the model-dependent magnitude caveat.\n\n- **N=8, T=1.0**: Only these primary settings are tested for selection. T=0.7 is tested for QE but not selection. N=16 is tested for QE but selection results are limited to cross-configuration tables showing the same null. The paper should discuss whether higher T or much larger N might unlock selection--the theoretical argument that more diversity could help is not addressed.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Missing supervised baseline in the main paper.** The paper identifies a 13-18pp oracle headroom gap but does not test whether ANY approach can close it. The Limitations section acknowledges \"We do not train a dedicated verifier or reward model.\" For a paper whose central narrative is \"these signals can't select,\" demonstrating what CAN select--even a simple supervised approach--would substantially strengthen the contribution. Without this, the paper documents a problem but offers no evidence about whether it is solvable.\n\n**W2. Single RE benchmark.** The paper repeatedly flags RE results as \"preliminary, single benchmark\" (appearing 5+ times). This is honest but limits the generalizability of approximately one-third of the claimed contributions. The RE conditional collapse, the LP-RE interaction in free-form decoding, and the RE miscalibration findings all rest on SciERC RE alone.\n\n**W3. Free-form ablation on single configuration.** The ablation is the paper's most important mechanistic finding, yet it covers only Qwen3-8B on SciERC. The RE interaction effect (LP selection flipping from -0.68pp to +1.69pp) is reported but unexplained, and without a second configuration, it is unclear whether this is a robust task-specific effect or noise.\n\n**W4. No discussion of when or why consistency signals succeed in MT/reasoning but fail here.** The MBR decoding connection is mentioned but not analyzed deeply. What structural property of IE outputs (as opposed to MT outputs) causes utility-quality misalignment within instances? Is it the discrete set structure? The combinatorial output space? The error correlation structure? The paper gestures at this (\"utility correlates with F1 across instances but not within them\") but does not provide a formal or empirical analysis of WHY.\n\n**W5. Somewhat predictable negative result.** For fine-tuned models generating near-identical structured outputs under constrained decoding, the finding that signals can't distinguish among very similar candidates is not highly surprising. The authors argue non-obviousness by analogy to MBR success in MT, but the analogy is imperfect (MT outputs are sequences with continuous similarity, not discrete sets). The paper would benefit from a stronger articulation of what specific prior work would have predicted success here.\n\n**W6. Title overclaims relative to evidence.** \"Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\" is a strong universal claim. The paper tests 2 models at 8B scale, 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset, and 1 decoding temperature. \"Cannot\" is too strong given this scope. \"Struggle to\" or \"fail to\" would be more appropriate.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Entropy-based signals**: Have you considered testing predictive entropy (sum of token-level entropies) or normalized entropy as an alternative per-sample signal? LP uses mean log-probability, but entropy captures uncertainty more directly and may be less compressed. If you have this data (since you collect token-level log-probs), this would be a low-cost but high-value addition.\n\n2. **Within-instance utility-quality correlation**: You state that MBR utility correlates with F1 across instances but not within them. Can you quantify the within-instance rank correlation between SJ-based consensus proximity score and per-sample F1? A distribution of within-instance Kendall tau values would make this claim precise.\n\n3. **Why does RE LP selection flip in free-form?** The constrained-to-free-form flip from -0.68pp to +1.69pp on RE is interesting but unexplained. Do you have any analysis of what changes structurally in RE outputs under free-form decoding? Is it output diversity, error type distribution, or something else?\n\n4. **Larger N for selection**: Your N-scaling analysis focuses on QE. At N=64 or N=128, the oracle headroom would be substantially larger. Would LP selection improve with more candidates, or does the compression bottleneck remain? Even a simulation (subsampling from N=16 to estimate N=64 behavior) would be informative.\n\n5. **The enable_thinking confound**: You acknowledge this for SciERC Qwen training. Is the greedy baseline (T=0) also affected? If the model was trained with empty think blocks but inference suppresses them, could this systematic train-test mismatch contribute to the compression effect? A simple diagnostic: does removing the SciERC Qwen configuration change any qualitative conclusions?\n\n---\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n**Soundness: 4 (Strong)**\nThe experimental design is rigorous, controls are appropriate, and statistical methodology is above average. The five signals are well-defined and the QE/selection separation is well-motivated. The free-form ablation is well-controlled. Weaknesses (single RE benchmark, single ablation configuration, missing supervised baseline) prevent a 5, but the core methodology is sound and the claims are appropriately hedged.\n\n**Excitement: 3 (Borderline)**\nThe correlation-selection gap is a useful conceptual contribution, and the systematic evaluation is thorough. However, the core finding--that unsupervised signals can't select among similar candidates from a fine-tuned model under constrained decoding--is moderately predictable. The paper documents a problem well but does not advance toward a solution. The difficulty-dependent signal ranking and WNUT-17 findings add interest but are secondary. For EMNLP Findings, this is adequate; for the main conference, the novelty bar would be tight.\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.5 (Leaning Accept)**\nA solid empirical contribution with thorough methodology, appropriate statistical rigor, and honest hedging of claims. The correlation-selection gap is a useful finding for the IE community, even if not highly surprising. The systematic signal comparison and difficulty-dependent analysis provide practical value. The paper is well-written (clear, well-organized, good use of appendix for detail). Weaknesses are primarily in scope (1 RE dataset, 1 ablation config, no supervised baseline) rather than methodology. Appropriate for EMNLP 2026 Findings as a careful empirical study that establishes an important negative result.\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\nI have read the full paper including all appendix sections, verified claims against reported numbers, and have relevant expertise in LLM-based IE and confidence estimation. I am less certain about the RE findings given the single-benchmark limitation, and I acknowledge uncertainty about whether larger models or different decoding strategies would alter the conclusions.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n- The conclusion file (`conclusion.tex`) and the `sections/conclusion.tex` differ: one mentions \"constrained-decoding-induced score compression\" and \"relaxed decoding constraints,\" while the other (correctly, per the ablation) says \"score compression\" without attributing it to constrained decoding. Ensure consistency.\n- Table 2 caption says \"RE results are from a single benchmark (preliminary)\" -- this caveat appears so frequently it becomes repetitive. State it once prominently and refer back.\n- The paper would benefit from a figure showing the LP score distribution (histogram of within-instance LP ranges) to make the compression claim visually immediate."}],"totalDurationMs":142592,"totalTokens":64110,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":861,"cache_read_input_tokens":59915,"output_tokens":3331,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":861},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3331,"cache_read_input_tokens":59915,"cache_creation_input_tokens":861,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":861,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a191732bade838085","tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7tZFLmL7vDnamgjTBwoNu","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":65881,"tool_uses":28,"duration_ms":203015},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"b7f7b00c-8f66-4eb9-b684-c6ea313e123f","session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015EfzrrP7Bgdos4f3rBaX8Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013QASzcx15wBH68T3RL1F6y","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper systematically evaluates five confidence signals from three families (structural, surface, model-internal) for quality estimation (QE) and sample selection in LLM-based information extraction under constrained decoding. The central finding is the \"correlation-selection gap\": consistency signals achieve strong instance-level QE (voting confidence rho=0.50, AUROC=0.73 on SciERC NER) but universally fail to improve best-of-N selection over greedy decoding. The paper evaluates across NER and RE on three datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17) with two 8B models (Qwen3, LLaMA-3.1), characterizes task-difficulty-dependent signal rankings, and identifies boundary conditions where all signals collapse.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n### 2a. The \"predict-but-cannot-select\" (correlation-selection gap) finding\n\n**Moderately novel, but partially expected.** The conceptual observation that instance-level aggregate signals cannot rank candidates within an instance is, as the authors themselves acknowledge in paper_framing.md, \"noted not discovered\" -- it follows definitionally from the fact that 4/5 signals produce a single scalar per instance. The more interesting empirical component is that logprob (the only per-sample signal) also fails on NER, and that the gap persists even under free-form decoding. However, the logprob failure is less surprising than presented: in a fine-tuned model producing near-deterministic outputs at high accuracy (greedy NER F1=0.61), logprob compression is an expected consequence of confident, well-calibrated fine-tuning. The paper would benefit from more explicitly engaging with the question of whether this finding would surprise a practitioner familiar with both self-consistency and structured prediction.\n\n**Strength**: The quantification of the gap with bootstrap CIs (p=0.39, Cohen's d=0.038) is rigorous. The oracle headroom analysis (NER +16pp, RE +18.7pp) effectively demonstrates that information exists but signals cannot access it.\n\n### 2b. QE-vs-selection conceptual distinction\n\n**Useful clarification, modest novelty.** Disentangling QE from selection is a genuine contribution to clarity in the IE confidence literature. However, this distinction is well-established in adjacent fields: quality estimation vs. reranking is a standard dichotomy in machine translation (Fomicheva et al. 2020, TACL), and selective prediction vs. best-of-N is implicit in the test-time scaling literature (Snell et al. 2024). The novelty lies in demonstrating that this distinction matters specifically for structured IE and explaining mechanistically why it does (instance-level aggregation vs. per-sample discrimination). This is incremental but useful.\n\n### 2c. Free-form ablation as diagnostic\n\n**The ablation result is genuinely informative.** The finding that removing constrained decoding does NOT recover logprob variance (std ratio 0.97) is a well-designed controlled experiment that falsifies a natural hypothesis. This is the most novel empirical contribution in the paper, as it rules out a plausible explanation and establishes that score compression is model/task intrinsic. However, this result appears only in the project framing documents and the ablation report -- it is NOT present in the current paper draft (the experiments.tex, discussion.tex, etc. do not contain the ablation results). If this is not included in the final submission, the paper loses its strongest novelty claim.\n\n### 2d. Supervised verifier baseline (DeBERTa)\n\n**Not yet available.** The abstract mentions \"supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom,\" but exp-019 is listed as \"waiting for approval\" and no results exist in the artifacts. If this is included in the final paper, it provides a useful \"so what\" anchor. A 15.9% headroom capture rate is modest but establishes that the gap is partially closable, transforming a pure negative result into an actionable one. The novelty of using a supervised verifier for IE selection is low (this is standard in the reasoning literature via Cobbe et al. 2021), but its application to structured IE is new.\n\n### 2e. Overall novelty verdict\n\nThis is best characterized as a **systematic empirical study that extends self-consistency to a new domain (structured IE) and identifies a non-trivial negative result**. It is not a methodological contribution -- no new method is proposed. The novelty is in the empirical finding (the gap), the diagnostic rigor (the ablation, the bootstrap analysis), and the practical characterization (task-difficulty-dependent signal rankings). For EMNLP Findings, this level of novelty is appropriate if the execution is clean. The paper does not claim to introduce a new method, and the framing as an empirical analysis paper is honest.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Related Work Coverage\n\n### 3a. Overall assessment\n\nThe related work section (as written in related_work.tex) is **comprehensive and well-organized**. The three-subsection structure (self-consistency/TTS, consistency for structured IE, uncertainty/calibration) covers the main threads effectively. The literature map contains 55 papers, and the paper cites 30+. This is adequate for EMNLP.\n\n### 3b. Key works cited and positioned\n\n- **Wang et al. 2023 (self-consistency)**: Properly cited as foundation, with clear differentiation (discrete answers vs. set-valued outputs). Good.\n- **CISC, Self-Certainty, ReASC**: All cited with explicit positioning. The dialogue with Self-Certainty (Kang et al.) in the introduction is effective -- \"reward-free selection succeeds for reasoning; our results demonstrate this does not transfer to structured extraction.\"\n- **Nedellec and Xia 2026**: Thoroughly discussed with three specific limitations (surface matching, no selection, single domain). The differentiation is convincing and fair.\n- **CoCoA (Vashurin et al. 2025a)**: Cited but discussed in only one sentence. Given that CoCoA is the closest methodological analog (MBR-based confidence+consistency fusion for UQ), it deserves more discussion. Specifically: how does CoCoA's MBR formulation relate to the paper's consensus-proximity analysis? Does the CoCoA framework predict the correlation-selection gap?\n- **Snell et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2024**: Properly cited as TTS foundations.\n- **Verifiers (Cobbe, Lightman)**: Cited as alternative paradigm. Adequate.\n\n### 3c. Critical missing references\n\n1. **Eikema and Aziz 2022 (MBR decoding)**: Listed in the abstract's reference set but I do not see it cited in related_work.tex. Given that the paper's SJ-based selection is conceptually related to MBR with set Jaccard as the utility function, MBR decoding should be explicitly discussed. The claim that \"set-based IE metrics create different optimization landscape where consensus-proximity fails\" needs MBR as the explicit foil.\n\n2. **Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al. 2024)**: Cited in introduction and related work, but the discussion could be deeper. USC uses the LLM itself as a judge to select the most consistent answer -- this is a viable alternative to the metric-based signals the paper evaluates. The paper should discuss why USC was not included as a baseline (presumably cost, but this should be stated).\n\n3. **Fomicheva et al. 2020 (Unsupervised QE for MT)**: In the literature map but not in the paper. This is the closest analog from MT -- using MC dropout and ensemble uncertainty for quality estimation without supervision. The QE-vs-selection distinction the paper emphasizes is well-established in MT QE literature, and acknowledging this precedent would strengthen rather than weaken the paper.\n\n4. **Structured prediction under constrained decoding**: The paper uses Outlines/XGrammar for constrained decoding but does not discuss the growing literature on how grammar constraints affect model distributions. Work on how constrained decoding distorts logprob distributions (e.g., Park et al. 2024 on grammar-constrained decoding effects) would directly contextualize the logprob compression finding.\n\n5. **GenIE (Josifoski et al. 2022)**: In the literature map and cited in the related work, but its bi-level constrained decoding approach is directly relevant to understanding how constraints affect confidence signals. Could be discussed more.\n\n### 3d. Is the claim \"none address structured IE\" accurate?\n\n**Mostly accurate, with a caveat.** The claim that no prior self-consistency work addresses structured IE outputs is true in the strict sense -- Wang et al., CISC, Self-Certainty, ReASC all target discrete answers. However, Nedellec and Xia 2026 do study consistency for IE (NER and RE), and Atomic SC (Thirukovalluru et al. 2024) addresses non-discrete structured outputs (atomic sub-claims). The paper should soften this to \"no prior work systematically compares confidence signal families for set-valued structured IE outputs or evaluates their selection utility\" rather than implying no one has studied consistency for IE at all.\n\n### 3e. Differentiation from Nedellec and Xia 2026\n\n**Convincing.** The three-point differentiation (surface matching vs. SJ, no selection analysis, single domain) is concrete and fair. The paper directly quotes Nedellec and Xia's own acknowledged limitation about overlooking semantically consistent outputs, which is effective positioning. This is well-handled.\n\n### 3f. CoCoA discussion sufficiency\n\n**Insufficient.** CoCoA is the most methodologically similar work -- it fuses model confidence (logprob) with inter-sample consistency via an MBR framework. The paper's ensemble of SJ+logprob (Table 3) is conceptually similar. The paper should discuss: (a) whether CoCoA's formulation would predict the correlation-selection gap, (b) why the ensemble underperforms the best single signal (EM), and (c) how CoCoA's success on QA/summarization/translation contrasts with the paper's failure on structured IE. This contrast would strengthen the \"structured IE is different\" argument.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Framing and Positioning\n\n### 4a. Venue fit\n\n**Good fit for EMNLP Findings.** The paper is a systematic empirical study with a well-characterized negative result. It does not propose a new method, which makes it marginal for main conference, but the diagnostic rigor and practical guidance are valuable for Findings. The comparison-plus-boundary-conditions contribution pattern is well-suited to this venue.\n\n### 4b. \"Correlation-selection gap\" terminology\n\n**Clear and useful.** The term is well-defined, easy to remember, and captures the phenomenon precisely. It has potential to become a standard reference term if the paper is published. Minor concern: the term appears only in the NER context where it is absolute (no signal beats greedy). On RE, logprob does improve over greedy (+2.3pp), so the \"gap\" is partial. The paper should acknowledge this more prominently -- the gap is task-dependent, not universal, despite the paper_framing.md claiming universality.\n\n### 4c. Three contributions -- scope vs. evidence\n\n- **Contribution 1 (first systematic comparison)**: Well-supported by the evidence. Five signals, three datasets, two models. The scope is appropriate.\n- **Contribution 2 (correlation-selection gap)**: Supported for NER, partially for RE. The bootstrap analysis (p=0.39) is rigorous. However, the claim would be stronger with the free-form ablation included in the paper (currently missing from the draft).\n- **Contribution 3 (task-complexity boundary)**: The weakest contribution. The EM-dominates-easy / SJ-dominates-hard / all-converge-when-insufficient characterization is useful but somewhat obvious -- simpler signals suffice for simpler tasks. The WNUT-17 boundary analysis (logprob rho=-0.411, overconfidence on novel entities) is more interesting and could be elevated.\n\n### 4d. Title\n\nThe current draft does not have a single consistent title. The abstract suggests the framing is about \"confidence signals for test-time scaling in generative IE.\" The paper_framing.md proposes \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\" The prompt's title is \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction.\"\n\nThe prompt's title includes \"Constrained-Decoding\" which is misleading if the ablation shows the gap is NOT constrained-decoding-specific. The paper_framing title \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors\" is more accurate and catchier. However, \"Cannot Scale\" overstates -- the paper shows signals cannot *select*, not that they cannot scale IE in general (e.g., QE-based routing could still help). I would recommend: **\"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select: The Correlation-Selection Gap in LLM-Based Information Extraction\"**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Potential Impact\n\n### 5a. Who benefits?\n\n- Practitioners deploying LLM-based IE systems who need to decide whether to invest in multi-sample generation and selection strategies. The paper's practical recommendation (use consistency for QE/routing, not for selection) is immediately actionable.\n- Researchers working on test-time scaling who need to understand domain boundaries for self-consistency methods.\n- Researchers developing confidence estimation for structured prediction tasks beyond IE.\n\n### 5b. Research directions opened\n\n- **Supervised verifiers for structured IE**: The oracle headroom analysis motivates training dedicated verifiers, analogous to the reasoning literature.\n- **Per-sample signals for structured outputs**: The paper identifies that instance-level signals cannot select, motivating research into per-sample structural quality signals.\n- **Constrained decoding effects on model distributions**: The ablation opens questions about when and how constrained decoding affects different aspects of generation quality.\n\n### 5c. Citation likelihood\n\n**Moderate.** The paper would likely be cited by: (a) future work on self-consistency for structured tasks, (b) papers on LLM-based IE that need to discuss confidence estimation, (c) test-time scaling papers that discuss domain limitations. The \"correlation-selection gap\" term could become a standard reference if the finding is robust. I estimate 15-30 citations in the first two years.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Weaknesses\n\n### W1. Novelty is primarily negative/empirical, not methodological.\nThe paper identifies a limitation (signals cannot select) but does not propose a solution. The free-form ablation and supervised verifier are diagnostic, not prescriptive. For Findings this is acceptable, but it limits impact.\n\n### W2. The free-form ablation -- the strongest novelty claim -- is missing from the paper draft.\nThe experiments.tex, discussion.tex, and conclusion.tex I reviewed do not contain the ablation results. The abstract mentions constrained decoding analysis, but the current paper body appears to be from an earlier draft (pre-D072). If this is not included in the final submission, the paper loses its most distinctive empirical contribution and reverts to a descriptive comparison study.\n\n### W3. Inconsistency between abstract/framing and paper body.\nThe abstract provided in the prompt mentions \"removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x\" and \"supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom.\" The paper_framing.md (D072) says the ablation FALSIFIED the 3.5x claim (actual ratio is 0.97x, NOT 3.5x). The abstract appears to be from an older version. Additionally, the supervised verifier (exp-019) has not been completed. This creates a coherence risk if the final submission mixes old and new claims.\n\n### W4. Single RE benchmark limits the correlation-selection gap generalizability claim.\nSciERC RE has 44% zero-F1 instances, creating a severe floor effect. The gap on RE may be partly an artifact of the model simply not being capable enough at this task. A second RE dataset (e.g., TACRED, DocRED) would substantially strengthen the claim.\n\n### W5. CoCoA and MBR under-discussed.\nAs noted in Section 3, CoCoA is the closest methodological analog and deserves deeper engagement. The SJ-based selection is essentially MBR with Jaccard utility, and the paper should make this connection explicit and explain why MBR fails here despite succeeding in MT.\n\n### W6. The \"universality\" claim requires nuance.\nThe paper_framing claims the gap is \"universal\" based on the free-form ablation. But \"universal\" is a strong word -- it has been demonstrated on one task family (IE), two datasets for NER, one for RE, with two 8B models. The gap may not hold for larger models, different IE formulations (e.g., code-generation-based IE), or different output structures (e.g., event extraction). \"Robust across our experimental conditions\" is more defensible than \"universal.\"\n\n### W7. Logprob RE selection gain (+2.3pp) somewhat undermines the gap narrative.\nThe paper frames the gap as the central finding, but logprob does help on RE. The narrative needs to more clearly articulate that the gap is absolute for NER but partial for RE, rather than presenting it as a universal failure. The current framing oscillates between \"no signal helps\" and \"logprob helps on RE.\"\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** The free-form ablation (exp_freeform_ablation) shows LP std ratio of 0.97x, falsifying the constrained decoding bottleneck hypothesis. But the abstract provided for review mentions \"removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x.\" Which is the correct claim for the final submission? This is a critical inconsistency that must be resolved.\n\n**Q2.** CoCoA (Vashurin et al. 2025a) unifies confidence and consistency via MBR, achieving strong results on QA and summarization. Your SJ-based selection is conceptually an MBR approach with Jaccard utility. Can you explain mechanistically why this MBR-like approach fails for IE when it succeeds for other generation tasks? Is it the discreteness of the output space, the set structure, or something else?\n\n**Q3.** The supervised verifier (DeBERTa, 15.9% headroom capture) is mentioned in the abstract but exp-019 appears incomplete. Will this be included in the final submission? If so, what is the training setup (how many labeled examples, cross-validation strategy)?\n\n**Q4.** You evaluate at N=8 and N=16. Given that RE signals improve substantially from N=8 to N=16 (voting rho from 0.340 to 0.448 per paper_framing.md), have you considered whether the correlation-selection gap might narrow at N=32 or N=64? The gap could be a sample budget artifact for RE rather than a fundamental limitation.\n\n**Q5.** The WNUT-17 finding (logprob rho=-0.411, strong negative correlation) is striking and arguably the most practically important result. Have you investigated whether this negative correlation is specific to novel/emerging entities, or whether it occurs for any OOD shift? This has implications for whether logprob should be used as a *negative* signal for abstention on OOD inputs.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Scores\n\n### Soundness: 3.5 / 5\n**Justification**: The experimental methodology is rigorous -- bootstrap CIs, multiple seeds, leave-one-out circularity checks, cross-model validation. The statistical analysis is appropriate (Spearman rho, AUROC, bootstrap p-values, Cohen's d). However, the inconsistency between the abstract (3.5x variance recovery) and the actual ablation result (0.97x ratio) raises concerns about which claims will appear in the final paper. The single RE dataset and the incomplete supervised verifier experiment also reduce confidence. If the final paper resolves these issues, soundness would be 4.0.\n\n### Excitement: 2.5 / 5\n**Justification**: The correlation-selection gap is a useful finding but not a surprising one. The definitional component (instance-level signals cannot rank per-sample) is straightforward. The empirical component (logprob also fails due to score compression) is more interesting but is partially expected for fine-tuned models. The free-form ablation proving intrinsic compression is the most exciting finding, but it is not in the current paper draft. Without the ablation and verifier, this is a competent empirical comparison study with a negative result -- useful for the community but not exciting. With the ablation included and well-framed, excitement would rise to 3.0.\n\n### Overall Assessment: 3.0 / 5 (Borderline)\n**Justification**: This is a well-executed empirical study that makes a useful contribution to the structured IE and test-time scaling communities. The QE evaluation framework is thorough, the cross-model and cross-dataset validation is appropriate for the scope, and the correlation-selection gap is clearly demonstrated. The paper is honest about its limitations. However, the novelty is incremental -- extending self-consistency analysis to a new domain and documenting where it fails. The lack of a proposed solution limits impact. The critical question for accept/reject is whether the free-form ablation and supervised verifier are included: with them, this is a solid Findings paper (3.5); without them, it is a descriptive comparison study that sits at the borderline (2.5-3.0). I score at 3.0 assuming partial inclusion of the ablation but noting the uncertainty.\n\nFor EMNLP Findings, I lean toward **borderline accept** contingent on: (1) including the free-form ablation results with correct numbers, (2) resolving the abstract inconsistency, (3) expanding CoCoA/MBR discussion, and (4) toning down universality claims.\n\n### Confidence: 4 / 5\nI am confident in this assessment. I have expertise in LLM uncertainty estimation and structured prediction, and I have reviewed the full paper draft, experimental results, and project documentation carefully. My main uncertainty is about what the final submission will contain, given the discrepancies between the abstract provided for review and the actual experimental results.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Borderline accept for EMNLP Findings**, pending resolution of the abstract/ablation inconsistency and inclusion of the free-form ablation as a first-class contribution. The paper's strengths are its systematic evaluation, rigorous statistical analysis, and the practically useful correlation-selection gap finding. Its weaknesses are limited novelty beyond the negative result, under-discussion of CoCoA/MBR, and an incomplete supervised verifier experiment. The free-form ablation is the paper's best claim to novelty and must be prominently included with correct numbers.\n","project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T092545","overall_score":"borderline accept"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_read_input_tokens":64434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":709,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7tZFLmL7vDnamgjTBwoNu","session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"a133b04e-1b71-48a1-bfd6-a9bc96984906"}
