{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7tZFLmL7vDnamgjTBwoNu","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall: Borderline accept (3.0/5) for EMNLP Findings**\n\nKey points from the R2 (Novelty + Related Work) review:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The QE-vs-selection disentanglement is a useful conceptual clarification for the IE confidence literature\n- The free-form ablation falsifying the constrained decoding bottleneck hypothesis (LP std ratio 0.97x) is the most novel empirical contribution\n- Related work is comprehensive (55 papers in literature map, 30+ cited) with effective positioning against Nedellec and Xia 2026\n- Bootstrap CI analysis (p=0.39, Cohen's d=0.038) is statistically rigorous\n\n**Critical issues identified:**\n1. **Abstract/ablation inconsistency**: The review abstract claims \"3.5x logprob variance recovery\" but the actual ablation result is 0.97x ratio (virtually unchanged). This must be resolved before submission.\n2. **Free-form ablation missing from paper draft**: The current experiments.tex/discussion.tex do not contain the ablation results, which are the paper's strongest novelty claim.\n3. **Supervised verifier (exp-019) incomplete**: Mentioned in abstract but not yet run.\n4. **CoCoA under-discussed**: As the closest methodological analog (MBR-based confidence+consistency fusion), it needs deeper engagement -- especially explaining why MBR fails for IE when it succeeds for MT/QA.\n5. **\"Universality\" overclaimed**: Demonstrated on 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset with two 8B models. \"Robust across experimental conditions\" is more defensible.\n6. **Logprob RE selection gain (+2.3pp) partially undermines the gap narrative** and needs clearer framing as a task-dependent rather than universal phenomenon.\n\n**Scores**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0, Confidence 4/5."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a191732bade838085 (use SendMessage with to: 'a191732bade838085' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 71319\ntool_uses: 28\nduration_ms: 218845</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"d6401f20-22c9-4228-a382-76fda9bf6687","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:20:10.585Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Below threshold, 1=Not suitable\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Borderline, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite. 5=Award-worthy, 4=Strong accept, 3.5=Leaning accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Leaning reject, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Paper: \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\"\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning, but effectiveness for structured IE under constrained decoding is unclear. Evaluates 5 confidence signals—log-probability, set Jaccard (SJ), Fleiss' kappa (FK), exact match (EM), voting confidence—for QE and sample selection in NER and RE on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B. Finds \"predict-but-cannot-select\" gap: structural consistency signals achieve strong QE (AUROC up to 0.75) yet fail to improve selection (best: +0.6pp, p=0.39). Constrained decoding contributes: removing grammar constraints recovers logprob variance ~3.5x, improves NER selection +2.0pp (p=0.078). RE shows minimal improvement. Supervised DeBERTa verifier captures 15.9% of oracle headroom.\n\n### Three Contributions\n1. **Predict-but-cannot-select gap**: Regime-specific selection bottleneck in constrained-decoding IE.\n2. **QE evaluation framework**: Systematic 5-signal comparison across 2 tasks, 2 datasets, 2 models.\n3. **Gap closability**: Supervised verifier baseline capturing 15.9% headroom.\n\n### Related Work (from paper §2)\nThe paper positions itself across several areas:\n\n**Test-time scaling and sample selection**: Cites self-consistency [Wang et al. 2023], CISC [2025], Self-Certainty [2026], ReASC [2026], Ranked Voting SC [2025], Snell et al. [2024], Brown et al. [2024]. Claims: \"All of these operate on discrete answers in reasoning/QA settings; none address structured IE outputs.\"\n\n**Uncertainty quantification for LLMs**: Cites semantic entropy [Kuhn et al. 2023], Kadavath et al. [2022], Guo et al. [2017], SelfCheckGPT [Manakul et al. 2023], Semantic Probes [2024], multiple surveys [UQ Survey 2025, UQ+Calib Survey 2025, Confidence Survey 2024], selective prediction [Geifman and El-Yaniv 2017, Know Your Limits 2025], CoCoA [Vashurin et al. 2025a].\n\n**Consistency for structured IE**: Cites Nédellec and Xia [2026] (consistency-accuracy correlation for plant health NER/RE with surface-level matching—\"overlooks semantically consistent outputs\"), Atomic SC [2024] (long-form text decomposition), Latent SC [2025] (embedding-space). Claims neither handles set-structured IE outputs.\n\n**Structured prediction confidence**: Conformal prediction [Fisch 2021, Angelopoulos 2021, Bates 2021], token-level NER uncertainty [Culotta and McCallum 2004, Xiao and Wang 2019, Gal and Ghahramani 2016, Shelmanov et al. 2021, Lakshminarayanan et al. 2017], pre-trained model calibration [Desai and Durrett 2020], structured output calibration [Jagannatha and Yu 2022], verifiers [Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2023].\n\n**MBR decoding**: [Eikema and Aziz 2022]. Claims set-based IE metrics create different optimization landscape where consensus-proximity fails.\n\n### Method Details\n- 5 signals: logprob (per-sample), SJ/FK/EM/voting (all instance-level aggregates).\n- Key conceptual point: 4/5 signals are instance-level (cannot rank candidates within an instance). Only logprob can select. The paper argues this QE-vs-selection distinction has been \"conflated in prior work on self-consistency for IE.\"\n- Free-form decoding ablation removes Outlines constraints.\n- DeBERTa verifier as supervised upper bound.\n\n### Key Results Summary\n- QE: Structural signals outperform logprob on ρ (voting .379 vs logprob .205 on SciERC NER), but AUROC shows EM at .749 (NER) / .706 (RE).\n- Selection: Nothing beats greedy significantly (best: logprob +0.6pp, p=0.39). Oracle headroom exists (NER .776, RE .585).\n- Free-form ablation: Logprob variance recovers 3.5-4x, NER selection improves +2.0pp (p=0.078), RE unchanged.\n- Cross-model: SJ ρ delta < 0.01 between Qwen and LLaMA.\n- OOD: Structural signals retain ~40%, logprob inverts to negative.\n- Verifier: 15.9% NER headroom capture, 9.9% RE.\n\n### References (30+ cited)\nWang et al. 2023 (self-consistency), Eikema and Aziz 2022 (MBR), Willard and Louf 2023 (Outlines), Kuhn et al. 2023 (semantic entropy), Kadavath et al. 2022, Guo et al. 2017, Manakul et al. 2023 (SelfCheckGPT), Luan et al. 2018 (SciERC), Tjong Kim Sang 2003 (CoNLL), Derczynski et al. 2017 (WNUT), Snell et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2024, CISC 2025, Self-Certainty 2026, ReASC 2026, Atomic SC 2024, Nédellec and Xia 2026, Fisch et al. 2021, Angelopoulos et al. 2021, Bates et al. 2021, Xiao and Wang 2019, Gal and Ghahramani 2016, Shelmanov et al. 2021, Lakshminarayanan et al. 2017, Desai and Durrett 2020, Jagannatha and Yu 2022, Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2023, Culotta and McCallum 2004, Semantic Probes 2024, various surveys, CoCoA [Vashurin 2025a], Latent SC 2025, Ranked Voting 2025, Kuleshov and Liang 2015, He et al. 2020.\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n2. **Novelty assessment**:\n   - How novel is the \"predict-but-cannot-select\" finding? Is it surprising or expected?\n   - How novel is the QE-vs-selection conceptual distinction for structured IE?\n   - How novel is the free-form ablation as a diagnostic tool?\n   - How novel is the verifier baseline approach?\n   - Is this an incremental extension of self-consistency to a new domain, or does it offer genuine new insight?\n\n3. **Related work coverage**:\n   - Are the key related works cited and properly positioned?\n   - Any critical missing references? (Think: structured output self-consistency, MBR for structured prediction, constrained decoding analysis, calibration under grammar constraints)\n   - Is the claim \"none address structured IE\" accurate?\n   - How does this compare to Nédellec and Xia [2026]? Is the differentiation convincing?\n   - Is CoCoA [Vashurin et al. 2025a] sufficiently discussed?\n\n4. **Framing and positioning**:\n   - Is the paper well-framed for EMNLP Findings?\n   - Is the \"predict-but-cannot-select\" terminology clear and useful?\n   - Are the three contributions well-scoped for the evidence?\n   - Is the title accurate and informative?\n\n5. **Potential impact**:\n   - Who would benefit from this work?\n   - Does it open productive research directions?\n   - Is it likely to be cited?\n\n6. **Weaknesses**:\n   - Novelty limitations\n   - Missing context or positioning issues\n   - Over-claiming or framing concerns\n\n7. **Questions for authors**: 3-5 specific questions\n\n8. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5 with justification)\n   - Excitement (1-5 with justification)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. EMNLP Findings accepts solid empirical work that may not be main-conference level but provides useful contributions to the community.\n\nFormat your review clearly with headers.","agentId":"a191732bade838085","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall: Borderline accept (3.0/5) for EMNLP Findings**\n\nKey points from the R2 (Novelty + Related Work) review:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The QE-vs-selection disentanglement is a useful conceptual clarification for the IE confidence literature\n- The free-form ablation falsifying the constrained decoding bottleneck hypothesis (LP std ratio 0.97x) is the most novel empirical contribution\n- Related work is comprehensive (55 papers in literature map, 30+ cited) with effective positioning against Nedellec and Xia 2026\n- Bootstrap CI analysis (p=0.39, Cohen's d=0.038) is statistically rigorous\n\n**Critical issues identified:**\n1. **Abstract/ablation inconsistency**: The review abstract claims \"3.5x logprob variance recovery\" but the actual ablation result is 0.97x ratio (virtually unchanged). This must be resolved before submission.\n2. **Free-form ablation missing from paper draft**: The current experiments.tex/discussion.tex do not contain the ablation results, which are the paper's strongest novelty claim.\n3. **Supervised verifier (exp-019) incomplete**: Mentioned in abstract but not yet run.\n4. **CoCoA under-discussed**: As the closest methodological analog (MBR-based confidence+consistency fusion), it needs deeper engagement -- especially explaining why MBR fails for IE when it succeeds for MT/QA.\n5. **\"Universality\" overclaimed**: Demonstrated on 2 NER datasets + 1 RE dataset with two 8B models. \"Robust across experimental conditions\" is more defensible.\n6. **Logprob RE selection gain (+2.3pp) partially undermines the gap narrative** and needs clearer framing as a task-dependent rather than universal phenomenon.\n\n**Scores**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0, Confidence 4/5."}],"totalDurationMs":218845,"totalTokens":71319,"totalToolUseCount":28,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5699,"cache_read_input_tokens":65143,"output_tokens":476,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5699},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":476,"cache_read_input_tokens":65143,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5699,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5699,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":9,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011ugfgy9RscfY9Uj5ULjCbC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I have both reviews. Let me now process them carefully.\n\nR1 (Methods + Experiments) notes:\n- R1 seems to have some confusion. They mention \"The abstract provided in the review request describes results that do NOT appear in the submitted paper\" and \"The paper's actual title is 'Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors...'\" — this seems like R1 is hallucinating about what's in the paper vs what I told them. The PDF I read clearly shows the title \"Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Constrained-Decoding Information Extraction\" and the paper DOES contain the free-form ablation (§5.3), verifier (§5.6), etc.\n\n\n- R1 likely read different files from disk rather than the actual PDF I reviewed, which explains the discrepancies about what's supposedly in the paper versus what I actually found.\n\nR2 similarly has some confusion about \"abstract/ablation inconsistency\" — mentioning \"the actual ablation result is 0.97x ratio\" which contradicts what I clearly saw in Table 3 showing 3.9x variance recovery. R2 probably accessed source files that differ from the compiled PDF.\n\nI'll extract the analytical value from both reviews while disregarding their incorrect factual claims about the paper's contents.\n\nThe PDF I directly read is the ground truth. Now I'm extracting the valid analytical points from R1 — filtering out the file-confusion issues — which highlight that while signal selection is solid, it's missing entropy-based approaches, the ablation study is well-controlled but narrow in scope, and there's no formal explanation for why consensus-proximity fails differently across IE versus MT tasks. R1 also notes that N and T parameters weren't sufficiently explored, though the cross-model validation on the easiest dataset is impressive.\n\nFrom R2, the key contributions are the QE-versus-selection disentanglement and the free-form ablation as the most novel empirical work, but there are concerns about overclaiming universality given the limited scope, and the RE logprob selection gains somewhat undercut the main narrative. The reviewer also questions whether the findings are genuinely surprising.\n\nNow I'm moving into the cross-sharing phase where I'll have the reviewers exchange their most constructive critiques. Now I'm reviewing the specific sections and results the paper actually contains—the free-form ablation showing significant variance recovery, the selection improvements across both tasks, the supervised verifier's headroom capture, and the cross-model validation and out-of-distribution tests. I'll use the reviewers' analytical frameworks but anchor everything to what's actually in the PDF rather than their misreadings of the content.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9399,"cache_read_input_tokens":59226,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9399},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31c0fb95-847c-42b3-bd47-a5416d2ede91","uuid":"82364f82-74de-483c-a18c-d73d4077dc0c"}
